  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given $f(x)=x+2$ and $g(x)=x-5$, find $(fg)(-1).$",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)+2=1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)-5=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=1\\cdot (-6)=-6\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(x+2)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-10 \\\\  & =1+3-10 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
